  "autoGen5513": "The TRIM policy is configured by default (that is, SetApfsTrimTimeout = -1)",
  "autoGen5514": "1. The APFS system performs TRIM operations based on the default TRIM timeout of macOS, which is applicable to most SSDs that support TRIM.",
  "autoGen5515": "2. On SSDs that support TRIM, macOS automatically manages the execution of TRIM, which helps clean up deleted data blocks, making SSD writing more efficient, avoiding frequent write amplification, and improving writing speed.",
  "autoGen5516": "3.TRIM will regularly clean up unused blocks to reduce SSD wear and tear, thus extending SSD service life.",
  "autoGen5517": "4. Some third-party SSDs that do not fully support TRIM may experience delays or even freezes when executing TRIM, causing the boot process to become very slow.",
  "autoGen5518": "Completely disable TRIM functionality (i.e. set SetApfsTrimTimeout = 0)",
  "autoGen5519": "Benefits of disabling TRIM:",
  "autoGen5520": "1. Improve boot speed. For third-party SSDs that do not support or partially support TRIM, macOS may cause delays due to trying TRIM operations during startup. Disabling TRIM can eliminate this delay and improve boot speed.",
  "autoGen5521": "2. Improve compatibility. Some SSDs with poor compatibility can reduce the risk of abnormal crashes.",
  "autoGen5522": "3. Extend the life of non-TRIM SSDs. On SSDs that do not support TRIM, forcibly enabling TRIM may accelerate wear and affect life. Disabling TRIM can extend the life of such SSDs.",
  "autoGen5523": "Disadvantages of disabling TRIM:",
  "autoGen5524": "1. SSD performance declines. Some SSDs do not have the TRIM garbage collection mechanism. The SSD cannot clean and mark free blocks in time after deleting data, which may cause the writing speed to slow down.",
  "autoGen5525": "2. Disk space management efficiency is reduced. After disabling it, the SSD controller requires more time and resources for garbage collection, which may lead to increased fragmentation and reduced space management efficiency.",
  "autoGen5526": "3. Shorten the life of the SSD. For SSDs that support TRIM, after disabling TRIM, the SSD cannot optimize the writing process, which will increase the wear of the storage blocks, thus shortening the life of the SSD that supports TRIM.",
  "autoGen5527": "Although disabling TRIM can bring some boot speed improvements, for modern SSDs that support TRIM, it is recommended to keep TRIM turned on for long-term performance and stability. This speed increase is usually small and will only have a significant effect on some incompatible SSDs.",
